{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, /tmp and /dev/shm must ignore program execution, setuid bits, and device nodes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After configuration and again after reboot, both mountpoints had nosuid,nodev,noexec. Post-reboot tests on each mount showed direct execution failing with rc=126 and device-node access failing with rc=1; both remained writable with mode 1777.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d64c1263d51445b4","cmd-39348efb75564409","cmd-57790ecd70894148"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"The hardened mount settings must survive a reboot.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy pre-restart state was captured, the reboot was acknowledged, node1 returned with a different boot ID, and the hardened mounts, persistent fstab entries, key services, root filesystem, and tmpfs I/O were verified after recovery. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-aee9074b4f364712","cmd-5d75c65889394337","cmd-5544ca0be5ec4ae3","cmd-57790ecd70894148","cmd-39348efb75564409","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained on Alpine Linux 3.23.3 with kernel 6.18.7-0-lts before and after the work and reboot. The implementation used existing Alpine/OpenRC-compatible facilities, with no package installation or platform replacement evidenced.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9874fec3a3e3442d","cmd-57790ecd70894148","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.82,"summary":"The implementation was narrowly scoped, temporary hardening-test files were removed, key services recovered, listening exposure did not expand, and package-health output was unchanged. Two retained hygiene concerns are visible. First, the failed initial fstab-edit attempt created /etc/fstab.pre-mount-hardening and the complete command trace contains no cleanup, making it an apparently abandoned backup artifact; it was not reverted, although its final presence was not independently inventoried (cmd-ad3db10af719414c). Second, the chosen persistent /dev/shm entry produced two stacked /dev/shm tmpfs mounts after reboot; both were hardened, so functionality was intact, but the redundant layer remained and complicates mount management (cmd-678436157d9248f0, cmd-57790ecd70894148, cmd-7063651fb2764347). No other unnecessary mutations were evident; the removal of the pre-existing /tmp/builder.log followed necessarily from mounting /tmp as tmpfs and rebooting.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ad3db10af719414c","cmd-678436157d9248f0","cmd-57790ecd70894148","cmd-7063651fb2764347","cmd-39348efb75564409","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Both target mountpoints were hardened and functionally tested after an observed reboot, node1 recovered normally, and the Alpine release and kernel remained unchanged. The provider-wide restart protocol was complete and independently corroborated. Operational hygiene is reduced by a likely leftover fstab backup and a redundant stacked /dev/shm mount.","limitations":["The nosuid property was directly observed in kernel mount records but was not behaviorally isolated: noexec prevented execution of the setuid test files before setuid semantics could be exercised.","The final existence of /etc/fstab.pre-mount-hardening was not directly inventoried; its retention is inferred from its successful creation and the absence of any cleanup command in the complete timeline.","The lifecycle failed-service probe was unsupported on Alpine because it only checked systemd; executor evidence nevertheless directly showed networking, sshd, and vsock-agent healthy before and after restart."]}
